model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "Stopped recovery is disabled and unbound from the deployed Worker. The optional lifecycle launch ledger records the exact controller, logical runtime, admitted application/version/image, release epoch, monotonic generation and unpredictable token before every managed start. The start-only action serializes behind all warm and manual restart work, refuses a running process or changed generation/target, validates a fresh exact stopped observation, and consumes the existing global automatic budget and started receipt before starting its bound runtime. A historical boot without a trusted current generation never authorizes a launch. Unknown outcomes remain started and inspection-only. Completion requires a new boot with the matching authenticated launch proof, independent live plugin health and every current native policy acknowledged while holding the membership queue through receipt persistence. Retired membership tombstones remain dirty denials; a restarted boot must acknowledge a fresh denial at each retained revision before completion, without requiring a clean positive acknowledgement or reactivating a deleted pool. Restore and proof adapters must use direct private runtime ports, never join the lifecycle warm promise. No unready destruction, physical placement adapter, rollout mutual exclusion, production binding or activation is included."
    }
  }
  extend basalt.poolRuntime {
    metadata {
      constraint "A restart is admitted, and its launch witness written, only after the container's own object reads it as not running twice across a confirmation wait of two seconds; one not-running read is no proof of an exit. A selected runtime whose container keeps running while it refuses as unhealthy, late, identity or uncertain for sixty seconds without a successful health read is stopped by that container's object, SIGTERM first and destroy only after a thirty-second grace, and the existing stopped-container restart replaces it. The stop never fights a fence, deselection, retirement, drain, candidate, promotion or handoff, never stops a runtime that answers health, waits for active streams for at most five minutes, and waits while Home reports a token refresh in flight. At most three forced restarts per hour are allowed, counted by the controller and again by the container's object before the signal. A failed stop, a forced restart that has not served within five minutes, a restart recovery admitted on its own that has not served within five minutes of the first such admission since the runtime last served, or a fourth forced restart in an hour records needs-a-person. Recovery keeps trying. A failed restart that recovery admitted on its own never blocks a forced stop. Needs-a-person is shown in the operator inspect and cleared when the runtime serves again or by the operator's reset. Health refusals, failed recovery passes, failed Home upkeep and install guard failures log the closed runtime refusal code."
      constraint "Optional launch generation and token inputs are validated together. Authenticated ready responses expose only the generation and token digest as headers while the existing independent policy-health lease is live. A healthy internal Home boot rotation may update the observed boot within the same authenticated launch; it cannot renew or replace the controller-owned launch generation or token. Ordinary warm and alarm starts refuse an unresolved automatic STARTED budget or an unacknowledged previous launch. Explicit recovery retains its existing manual or automatic guard authority. Neither the raw token nor launch metadata enters logs or the existing readiness body. Legacy startup omits both inputs and retains the same readiness contract."
      constraint "The controller arms its own upkeep alarm whenever none is set and a runtime is selected: on its first event after a code update or eviction, and on every fetch, release handoff, readiness read and availability probe, at most once every five seconds per isolate, and not while an alarm handler or a Home pass runs, since each re-arms its own deadline when it ends. Both the fleet and the Home deadline then run at once, in one write; an alarm another writer moved later than a stored deadline is lowered back to it, so an alarm lost to a handler that failed past the platform's retries, or one never armed, cannot end recovery and membership upkeep, and a healing release's own calls start the heal. With nothing selected it arms nothing, as before: there is nothing to recover, the adopt that selects a runtime arms the fleet itself, and a Home pass inside the call that built the object would move the pool's change revisions under a reader comparing them around its query."
    }
  }
}
